{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
# How FastAPI code reaches for `settings`
{% else %}
# How Flask code reaches for `settings`
{% endif %}

Every consumer imports the same module-level singleton:

```python
from {{ package_name }}.core.config import settings
```

{% if framework == "fastapi" %}
## FastAPI: `settings` is used directly, everywhere

FastAPI never wraps `settings` in anything — every consumer just
imports the object and reads attributes off it:

- `main.py` builds the app from it directly:
  `app = FastAPI(title=settings.app_name, debug=settings.debug{% if template == "rest-api" %}, lifespan=lifespan{% endif %})`.
{% if template == "rest-api" %}
- `core/db.py` builds the engine from it:
  `engine = create_async_engine(settings.database_url, echo=settings.debug)`.
{% if redis %}
- `core/redis.py` builds the client from it:
  `redis_client = Redis.from_url(settings.redis_url, decode_responses=True)`.
{% endif %}
{% if worker in ("taskiq", "celery") %}
- `worker.py` builds the broker from it (`settings.{% if broker == "rabbitmq" %}rabbitmq_url{% else %}redis_url{% endif %}`).
{% endif %}
{% if migrations %}
- `alembic/env.py` reads `settings.database_url` too, so migrations
  always target the same database the app itself would connect to —
  never a second, hand-copied connection string.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

There's no framework-level indirection to work around: if you need a
config value in a new module, import `settings` the same way and read
the attribute. Nothing here is request-scoped or needs `Depends()` —
see the `fastapi` skill's notes on why `Depends()` is for per-request
values, not app-lifetime singletons like this one.
{% else %}
## Flask: app-level values get copied onto `app.config`, everything else imports `settings` directly

Flask can't take a `Settings` instance as `Flask(__name__)`'s first
argument, so{% if template == "rest-api" %} `main.py`'s `create_app()` factory{% else %} `main.py`{% endif %} copies the handful of
values Flask itself{% if template == "rest-api" %} (or Flask-SQLAlchemy){% endif %} needs onto the app's own `config` mapping:

```python
{% if template == "rest-api" %}
def create_app(*, database_url: str | None = None, testing: bool = False) -> Flask:
    app = Flask(__name__)
    app.config["APP_NAME"] = settings.app_name
    app.config["DEBUG"] = settings.debug
    app.config["TESTING"] = testing
{% if database != "none" %}
    app.config["SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI"] = database_url or settings.database_url
{% endif %}
    ...
{% else %}
app = Flask(__name__)
app.config["APP_NAME"] = settings.app_name
app.config["DEBUG"] = settings.debug
{% endif %}
```

This is a one-time copy at app-construction time, not a live view —
the app never holds a reference to `settings` itself, it holds copies
of the values it needed. That's *only* different from FastAPI's
"keep the object" style because `Flask.__init__`/`app.config` is a
plain dict-like mapping with no constructor hook for an arbitrary
settings object; it isn't a sign that Flask's config is more dynamic
or re-read per request — `settings` itself is still built once, just
like in FastAPI.
{% if template == "rest-api" %}
Notice `create_app(*, database_url: str | None = None, ...)` accepts an
**override** for the database URL, defaulting to `settings.database_url`
only when the caller doesn't pass one. `tests/conftest.py` uses exactly
this to force an isolated in-memory SQLite database
(`create_app(database_url="sqlite:///:memory:", testing=True)`) without
touching `.env` or monkeypatching `settings` at all — prefer this same
explicit-override shape for any other test that needs a different
config value than what `.env` provides, rather than mutating the
shared `settings` singleton.

Everything that *isn't* Flask/Flask-SQLAlchemy itself skips
`app.config` entirely and imports `settings` directly, exactly like
FastAPI does:
{% if redis %}
- `core/redis.py`: `redis_client = Redis.from_url(settings.redis_url, decode_responses=True)`.
{% endif %}
{% if worker == "celery" %}
- `worker.py`: `celery_app = Celery(..., broker=settings.{% if broker == "rabbitmq" %}rabbitmq_url{% else %}redis_url{% endif %}, ...)`.
{% endif %}
{% if migrations %}
- `alembic/env.py`: `config.set_main_option("sqlalchemy.url", settings.database_url)`.
{% endif %}
So the rule of thumb: **inside the app factory**, app/Flask-specific
values go on `app.config`; **everywhere else** (workers, migrations,
standalone clients), import `settings` directly — don't invent a third
way to thread a config value into a new module.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}
